NNAAMMEE
     ssnnmmppdd..ccoonnff — SNMP daemon configuration file

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     ssnnmmppdd..ccoonnff is the configuration file for the snmpd(8) daemon.

     The ssnnmmppdd..ccoonnff file is divided into the following main sections:

     _M_a_c_r_o_s
           Definitions of variables that can be used later, simplifying the
           configuration file.

     _G_l_o_b_a_l _c_o_n_f_i_g_u_r_a_t_i_o_n
           Global runtime settings for snmpd(8).

     _U_s_e_r _c_o_n_f_i_g_u_r_a_t_i_o_n
           USM user definitions.

     _O_I_D _c_o_n_f_i_g_u_r_a_t_i_o_n
           Custom configuration of SNMP object identifiers and values.

     The current line can be extended over multiple lines using a backslash
     (‘\’).  Comments can be put anywhere in the file using a hash mark (‘#’),
     and extend to the end of the current line.  Care should be taken when
     commenting out multi-line text: the comment is effective until the end of
     the entire block.

     Argument names not beginning with a letter, digit, or underscore must be
     quoted.

     Additional configuration files can be included with the iinncclluuddee keyword,
     for example:

           include "/etc/snmpd.conf.local"

   MMaaccrrooss
     A macro is defined with a command of the form _n_a_m_e=_v_a_l_u_e.  The macro _n_a_m_e
     can contain letters, digits, and underscores and cannot be a reserved
     word (for example, ccoommmmuunniittyy, ssyysstteemm, or ooiidd).  Within unquoted argu‐
     ments, the string $_n_a_m_e is later expanded to _v_a_l_u_e.

     For example:

           ext_addr="192.168.0.1"
           listen on $ext_addr

   GGlloobbaall ccoonnffiigguurraattiioonn
     The following options can be set globally:

     bblloocckklliisstt _o_i_d
             Remove the _o_i_d subtree from view.  Multiple bblloocckklliisstt statements
             are supported.

     ffiilltteerr--rroouutteess (yyeess | nnoo)
             If set to yyeess, ask the kernel to filter route update messages on
             the routing socket.  Routing table information will not be avail‐
             able, but CPU use will be reduced during bulk updates.  The de‐
             fault is nnoo.

     lliisstteenn oonn [ttccpp | uuddpp] _a_d_d_r_e_s_s [ppoorrtt _p_o_r_t] [_f_l_a_g_s]
             Specify the local _a_d_d_r_e_s_s snmpd(8) should listen on for incoming
             SNMP messages, or aannyy to listen on all local IPv4 and IPv6 ad‐
             dresses.  Multiple lliisstteenn oonn statements are supported.  If no
             lliisstteenn oonn statement is present, the default is lliisstteenn oonn aannyy.

             The _f_l_a_g_s are as follows:

             rreeaadd    Accept get, getnext and bulkget requests.

             wwrriittee   Accepts set requests.

             nnoottiiffyy  Accepts trapv1 and trapv2 requests.

             ssnnmmppvv11  Enable SNMPv1 subsystem on the listen address.

             ssnnmmppvv22cc
                     Enable SNMPv2c subsystem on the listen address.

             ssnnmmppvv33  Enable SNMPv3 subsystem on the listen address.

             The default protocol is uuddpp.  The default _p_o_r_t is 161, unless
             nnoottiiffyy is the only permission flag; which sets the _p_o_r_t to 162.
             If no permission flags are specified it defaults to “rreeaadd wwrriittee”,
             or nnoottiiffyy when _p_o_r_t is 162.  If no subsystem flags are specified,
             it defaults to ssnnmmppvv33.

             Having nnoottiiffyy set requires at least one ttrraapp hhaannddllee statement.

     aaggeennttxx [ppaatthh _p_a_t_h] [oowwnneerr _o_w_n_e_r] [ggrroouupp _g_r_o_u_p] [mmooddee _m_o_d_e]
             Set up an agentx master socket at _p_a_t_h and defaults to
             _/_v_a_r_/_a_g_e_n_t_x_/_m_a_s_t_e_r.  Socket owner, group, and permissions can be
             set with _o_w_n_e_r, _g_r_o_u_p, and _m_o_d_e respectively and defaults to
             root, _agentx, and 660.  Multiple aaggeennttxx statements are sup‐
             ported.  Only unix sockets are supported.

     eennggiinneeiidd [ppeenn _e_n_t_e_r_p_r_i_s_e] _f_o_r_m_a_t
             Set the snmp engineid, used for instance identification and key
             generation for the uusseerr _a_u_t_h and _k_e_y.  _e_n_t_e_r_p_r_i_s_e specifies the
             private enterprise number of the instance and can be either an
             integer or ooppeennbbssdd (default).

             _f_o_r_m_a_t can be one of the following:

             iippvv44 _a_d_d_r_e_s_s
                     The engineID's format identifier is set to 1 and the ipv4
                     _a_d_d_r_e_s_s is used in the format.

             iippvv66 _a_d_d_r_e_s_s
                     The engineID's format identifier is set to 2 and the ipv6
                     _a_d_d_r_e_s_s is used in the format.

             mmaacc _a_d_d_r_e_s_s
                     The engineID's format identifier is set to 3 and the mac
                     _a_d_d_r_e_s_s is used in the format.

             tteexxtt _t_e_x_t
                     The engineID's format identifier is set to 4 and the
                     ASCII _t_e_x_t is used in the format.

             oocctteettss _o_c_t_e_t_s_t_r_i_n_g
                     The engineID's format identifier is set to 5 and the
                     _o_c_t_e_t_s_t_r_i_n_g in hexadecimal is used in the format.

             hhoosstthhaasshh [_h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e]
                     The engineID's format identifier is set to 129 and the
                     first 27 bytes of the sha256 hash of the _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e are
                     used in the format.  This option is only valid for
                     _e_n_t_e_r_p_r_i_s_e ooppeennbbssdd.  If used for the local engineID, then
                     _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e defaults to the value of hostname(1).  This for‐
                     mat is the default.

             _n_u_m_b_e_r _o_c_t_e_t_s_t_r_i_n_g
                     The engineID's format identifier is set to _n_u_m_b_e_r and the
                     _o_c_t_e_t_s_t_r_i_n_g in hexadecimal is used in the format.  This
                     format is only available if _e_n_t_e_r_p_r_i_s_e is not ooppeennbbssdd.

             aaggeennttiidd _o_c_t_e_t_s_t_r_i_n_g
                     RFC1910 legacy format.  _o_c_t_e_t_s_t_r_i_n_g must be 8 bytes (or
                     16 characters in hexadecimal format).

     mmiibb ddiirreeccttoorryy _p_a_t_h
             Specify which directories to recursively search for MIB files.
             Multiple directories can be specified.  If no directory is speci‐
             fied it defaults to _/_u_s_r_/_s_h_a_r_e_/_s_n_m_p_/_m_i_b_s.

     rreeaadd--oonnllyy ccoommmmuunniittyy _s_t_r_i_n_g
             Specify the name of the read-only community.  There is no default
             value.

     rreeaadd--wwrriittee ccoommmmuunniittyy _s_t_r_i_n_g
             Specify the name of the read-write community.  There is no de‐
             fault value.

     sseecclleevveell (nnoonnee | aauutthh | eenncc)
             Specify the lowest security level that snmpd(8) accepts on SN‐
             MPv3:

                  nnoonnee  Both authentication and encryption of messages is op‐
                        tional.

                  aauutthh  Authentication of messages is mandatory.  snmpd(8)
                        will discard any messages that don't have a valid di‐
                        gest.  Encryption of messages is optional.

                  eenncc   Messages must be encrypted and must have a valid di‐
                        gest for authentication.  Otherwise they will be dis‐
                        carded.  This is the default value.

     ssyysstteemm ccoonnttaacctt _s_t_r_i_n_g
             Specify the name or description of the system contact, typically
             a name or an email address.  The default value is _r_o_o_t_@_h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e
             using the hostname of the local machine.

     ssyysstteemm ddeessccrriippttiioonn _s_t_r_i_n_g
             Specify a description of the local system.  The default value is
             the operating system identification as printed by the uname(1)
             command using the --aa flag:

                   OpenBSD myhost.example.com 4.2 GENERIC#595 i386

     ssyysstteemm llooccaattiioonn _s_t_r_i_n_g
             Specify the string describing the location of the local system,
             typically a physical location.  The default value is an empty
             string.

     ssyysstteemm nnaammee _s_t_r_i_n_g
             Specify the name of the local system, typically a fully-qualified
             domain name.  The default value is the hostname of the local sys‐
             tem.

     ssyysstteemm ooiidd _o_i_d_-_s_t_r_i_n_g
             Specify the authoritative identification of the local system.
             The default value is _1_._3_._6_._1_._4_._1_._3_0_1_5_5_._2_3_._1
             (iso.org.dod.internet.private.enterprises.openbsd.23.1) identify‐
             ing a common OpenBSD system.

     ssyysstteemm sseerrvviicceess _n_u_m_b_e_r
             Specify a magic value which indicates the set of services that
             the local system may provide.  Refer to the _s_y_s_S_e_r_v_i_c_e_s descrip‐
             tion in the SNMP MIB for details.  The value is given in decimal.

     ttrraapp ccoommmmuunniittyy _s_t_r_i_n_g
             Specify the name of the trap community.  There is no default
             value.

     ttrraapp hhaannddllee _o_i_d "_c_o_m_m_a_n_d"
             Execute ccoommmmaanndd upon receipt of an SNMP trap that begins with a
             prefix of ooiidd.  Alternately, the string "ddeeffaauulltt" may be used, in
             which case the prefix used is 11..33.  The invoked _c_o_m_m_a_n_d will re‐
             ceive the following information about the trap on standard input,
             one per line, in this order: the resolved hostname of the host
             sending the trap, the IP address of the host sending the trap,
             and any variable bindings contained in the trap (the OID followed
             by the value, separated by a single space).  This option requires
             at least one lliisstteenn oonn statement with a nnoottiiffyy flag set.  Traps
             over SNMPv3 are currently unsupported.

     ttrraapp rreecceeiivveerr _a_d_d_r_e_s_s [ooiidd _o_i_d_-_s_t_r_i_n_g] ssnnmmppvv22cc [ccoommmmuunniittyy _s_t_r_i_n_g]
             [ssoouurrccee--aaddddrreessss _a_d_d_r_e_s_s]
             Specify the _a_d_d_r_e_s_s or FQDN of a remote trap receiver for outgo‐
             ing traps sent by snmpd(8).  This option may be specified multi‐
             ple times.  The daemon will send outgoing traps in ssnnmmppvv22cc for‐
             mat.  The default community is specified by the global ttrraapp
             ccoommmmuunniittyy option.  The IPv4 or IPv6 source address of the traps
             can be enforced using

     ttrraapp rreecceeiivveerr _a_d_d_r_e_s_s [ooiidd _o_i_d_-_s_t_r_i_n_g] [ssnnmmppvv33] uusseerr _n_a_m_e [sseecclleevveell
             _l_e_v_e_l] [ssoouurrccee--aaddddrreessss _a_d_d_r_e_s_s]
             Specify the _a_d_d_r_e_s_s or FQDN of a remote trap receiver for outgo‐
             ing traps sent by snmpd(8).  This option may be specified multi‐
             ple times.  The daemon will send outgoing traps in ssnnmmppvv33 format.
             uusseerr must point to an existing global uusseerr.  If sseecclleevveell is not
             defined, it defaults to the global sseecclleevveell option.  The IPv4 or
             IPv6 source address of the traps can be enforced using
             ssoouurrccee--aaddddrreessss.

   UUsseerr ccoonnffiigguurraattiioonn
     Users for the SNMP User-based Security Model (USM, RFC 3414) must be de‐
     fined in the configuration file:

     uusseerr _n_a_m_e [aauutthhkkeeyy _k_e_y aauutthh _h_m_a_c] [eenncckkeeyy _k_e_y eenncc _c_i_p_h_e_r]
           Defines a known user.  The aauutthhkkeeyy keyword is required to specify
           the digest key used to authenticate messages.  If this keyword is
           omitted then authentication is disabled for this user account.  Op‐
           tionally the HMAC algorithm used for authentication can be speci‐
           fied.  _h_m_a_c must be either hhmmaacc--mmdd55, hhmmaacc--sshhaa11, hhmmaacc--sshhaa222244,
           hhmmaacc--sshhaa225566, hhmmaacc--sshhaa338844, or hhmmaacc--sshhaa551122.  If omitted, the default
           is hhmmaacc--sshhaa11.

           With eenncckkeeyy the encryption key used to encrypt and decrypt messages
           for privacy is defined.  Without an eenncckkeeyy specification the user
           account will neither accept encrypted incoming messages nor will it
           encrypt outgoing messages.  The _e_n_c algorithm can be either ddeess or
           aaeess and defaults to aaeess.

           Any user account that has encryption enabled requires authentica‐
           tion to be enabled too.

   OOIIDD ccoonnffiigguurraattiioonn
     It is possible to specify user-defined OIDs in the configuration file:

     ooiidd _o_i_d_-_s_t_r_i_n_g nnaammee _n_a_m_e [rreeaadd--oonnllyy | rreeaadd--wwrriittee] [_t_y_p_e] _v_a_l_u_e
             Return the specified value to the client for this OID.  The
             rreeaadd--wwrriittee option may allow the client to override it, and the
             type is either ssttrriinngg or iinntteeggeerr.

FFIILLEESS
     /etc/snmpd.conf           Default location of the configuration file.
     /etc/examples/snmpd.conf  Example configuration file.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     The following example will tell snmpd(8) to listen on localhost for SN‐
     MPv2c messages only with the community “8LHQtm1QLGzk”, override the de‐
     fault system OID, set the magic services value, and provide some custom
     OID values:

           listen on 127.0.0.1 snmpv2c
           read-only community 8LHQtm1QLGzk

           system oid 1.3.6.1.4.1.30155.23.2
           system services 74

           oid 1.3.6.1.4.1.30155.42.1 name myName read-only string "humppa"
           oid 1.3.6.1.4.1.30155.42.2 name myStatus read-only integer 1

     The next example will enforce SNMPv3 with authenticated and encrypted
     communication and the user-based security model.  The configuration de‐
     fines several users using varying encryption and authentication algo‐
     rithms.

           seclevel enc

           user "mgmt" auth hmac-sha256 authkey "password123" enc aes enckey "321drowssap"
           user "hans" auth hmac-sha1 authkey "password456" enc aes enckey "654drowssap"
           user "sophie" auth hmac-md5 authkey "password789" enc des enckey "987drowssap"

